<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tisquantum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1585–1622</span> · Also known as Squanto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tisquantum, better known as Squanto, was a Patuxet man who acted as interpreter and guide for the Pilgrims of Plymouth Colony. Kidnapped by the English captain Thomas Hunt in 1614 and sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and returned home in 1619 to find his people wiped out by epidemic. He helped arrange the 1621 alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massasoit">Massasoit</a> and the colonists, taught them to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and died of illness in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tisquantum.jpg" alt="Tisquantum" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The German Kali Works, New York · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Squantoteaching.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
